<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Istiqlal Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1944">1944</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Independence Party (Morocco)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Istiqlal ('Independence') Party is a Moroccan nationalist party founded in 1944, when it issued a manifesto demanding independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Led by Allal al-Fassi and Ahmed Balafrej, it worked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-v-of-adal">Sultan Muhammad V</a> and led the struggle that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1956. It remains one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>'s main conservative parties.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/istiqlal-party.jpg" alt="Istiqlal Party" width="294" height="385" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Parti de l'Istiqlalحزب الإستقلال · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Logo_-_Parti_de_l%27Istiqlal.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berber-dahir">Berber Dahir</a> of 1930, which set up separate judicial systems for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a>, was a catalyst for Moroccan nationalism. Nationalists such as Ahmed Balafrej and Allal al-Fassi began to coordinate, and in 1934 the Moroccan Action Committee, also called the Kutla, published a Plan of Reforms that sought reform rather than independence. The Istiqlal Party was formed by former members of the committee, with Balafrej as head and al-Fassi in the honorary role of leader.</p><p>On 11 January 1944 it published its Independence Manifesto, which called for the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> under Sultan Muhammad bin Yusuf and a democratic constitutional government. It had between 56 and 59 signatories, among them Mehdi Ben Barka and Abderrahmane Youssoufi, and Malika al-Fassi was the only woman.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The French reacted to the manifesto with hostility, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> they arrested nationalists including Balafrej, which led to demonstrations, violence and further arrests. Membership was estimated at three thousand in 1944 and ten thousand in 1947. After the French deposed Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-v-of-morocco">Mohammed V</a>, the Istiqlal presented his return as a religious duty. Al-Fassi, in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, called for armed struggle by radio, and the party founded a resistance group, the Secret Organisation.</p><p>After independence the party was the largest in the Moroccan majlis and sought a government of its own, which brought it into conflict with the monarchy. By 1956 a left wing around Ben Barka and a right wing around al-Fassi and Balafrej had formed. In 1958 Balafrej became prime minister. After the left wing broke away in the summer of 1958, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammed-v-of-morocco">Mohammed V</a> made the trade unionist Abdallah Ibrahim prime minister, and the left formed the National Union of Popular Forces in 1959. Through the 1960s the Istiqlal was in opposition to Hassan II, though it supported the constitution of 1962. In 1998 it joined the Alternance government with the USFP.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The party's early leaders came largely from merchant families and religious scholars of Fes, joined by teachers and doctors and later by workers. Its ideology, which it calls Istiqlalism, was rooted in reformist Salafism and Arab nationalism, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> orders and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> tribal leaders regarded it with suspicion. After independence al-Fassi proposed the idea of Greater Morocco and published a map of it in the party newspaper Al-Alam in July 1956.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Istiqlal Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Istiqlal Party</b>, founded in 1943, led the nationalist movement and lobbied at the United Nations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-morocco">Kingdom of Morocco · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Berber Dahir of 1930 sparked nationalist protest, and the <b>Istiqlal</b> party demanded independence in 1944.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-protectorate-in-morocco">French protectorate in Morocco</a></li></ul></section>
